1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,451
Ever bent a paper clip back and forth until it snapped?

2
00:00:03,451 --> 00:00:06,372
The force was small, yet the metal failed.

3
00:00:06,372 --> 00:00:07,119
Why?

4
00:00:07,119 --> 00:00:09,076
The answer is fatigue failure.

5
00:00:09,076 --> 00:00:12,635
Fatigue happens when a material is loaded repeatedly.

6
00:00:12,635 --> 00:00:18,423
A component can survive a large force once, but break after many smaller forces.

7
00:00:18,423 --> 00:00:20,978
Damage builds up one cycle at a time.

8
00:00:20,978 --> 00:00:22,663
Picture a bicycle wheel.

9
00:00:22,663 --> 00:00:26,073
Each rotation changes the forces on its spokes.

10
00:00:26,073 --> 00:00:29,918
One complete loading and unloading is called a cycle.

11
00:00:29,918 --> 00:00:34,252
Repeated cycles can gradually create microscopic damage.

12
00:00:34,252 --> 00:00:37,730
Fatigue failure usually follows three stages.

13
00:00:37,730 --> 00:00:42,635
First, a tiny crack forms near a scratch, corner, or defect.

14
00:00:42,635 --> 00:00:45,883
Next, repeated loads make the crack longer.

15
00:00:45,883 --> 00:00:50,584
Finally, the remaining metal cannot support the load, and it breaks.

16
00:00:50,584 --> 00:00:55,353
Watch the strip as the load is applied, released, then applied again.

17
00:00:55,353 --> 00:00:58,220
The crack slowly grows with each cycle.

18
00:00:58,220 --> 00:01:03,410
It may be almost invisible, even as the component moves closer to failure.

19
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:08,355
After a real component breaks, engineers examine the fracture surface.

20
00:01:08,355 --> 00:01:12,513
Curved beach marks may reveal how the crack advanced over time.

21
00:01:12,513 --> 00:01:15,665
A rougher region can show the final sudden break.

22
00:01:15,665 --> 00:01:19,442
These patterns help engineers identify where the crack began.

23
00:01:19,442 --> 00:01:23,899
To estimate fatigue life, engineers use an S-N diagram.

24
00:01:23,899 --> 00:01:28,641
S stands for stress amplitude: the size of the repeated stress.

25
00:01:28,641 --> 00:01:31,874
N stands for the number of cycles until failure.

26
00:01:31,874 --> 00:01:35,271
Together, they describe how stress affects life.

27
00:01:35,271 --> 00:01:40,461
Notice the trend: high stress usually means fewer cycles before failure.

28
00:01:40,461 --> 00:01:43,192
Lower stress often means longer life.

29
00:01:43,192 --> 00:01:49,877
For some materials, particularly many steels, a threshold called the endurance limit may exist.

30
00:01:49,877 --> 00:01:53,315
This simplified curve is not experimental data.

31
00:01:53,315 --> 00:01:56,372
But fatigue life is only part of the story.

32
00:01:56,372 --> 00:02:01,453
Fracture toughness describes a material's resistance to crack-driven fracture.

33
00:02:01,453 --> 00:02:05,855
As a crack grows, stress becomes concentrated near its tip.

34
00:02:05,855 --> 00:02:09,864
Eventually, that crack may become dangerously unstable.

35
00:02:09,864 --> 00:02:15,815
Engineers describe crack-tip loading using stress intensity, represented by K.

36
00:02:15,815 --> 00:02:22,133
In a simplified case, K increases with applied stress and the square root of crack length.

37
00:02:22,133 --> 00:02:26,358
When K reaches a critical value, rapid fracture can begin.

38
00:02:26,358 --> 00:02:28,668
So how can we prevent fatigue failure?

39
00:02:28,668 --> 00:02:30,529
Reduce repeated stress.

40
00:02:30,529 --> 00:02:32,133
Avoid sharp corners.

41
00:02:32,133 --> 00:02:34,103
Choose suitable materials.

42
00:02:34,103 --> 00:02:36,807
Inspect important components regularly.

43
00:02:36,807 --> 00:02:41,453
The goal is to find and manage small cracks before they become dangerous.

44
00:02:41,453 --> 00:02:47,744
The key idea is simple: repeated loading can start a crack, and each cycle may help it grow.

45
00:02:47,744 --> 00:02:57,676
The S-N diagram describes fatigue life; the endurance limit applies to some materials; and fracture toughness helps explain sudden fracture.

46
00:02:57,676 --> 00:02:58,872
Thanks for watching.

